  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:JgtTzfmCUZM",
    "purpose": "Announces and presents a limited-run knife Kenji co-designed with bladesmith Gordon Hanson of Vonhelmick. He explains how the collaboration began, the blade design, the aesthetics, the materials and specifications, and how to buy one.",
    "video_type": "other",
    "thesis": "The Kenji x Vonhelmick knife is a hand-forged, santoku-based blade with a tapered gyuto-like tip and extra curve. It is meant to combine santoku chopping with western rocking cuts in one balanced, beautiful everyday knife.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "The knife grew out of a knife Kenji forged with Gordon Hanson after staying at his property in Oregon.",
      "The design starts from a Japanese santoku but tapers to a point like a western chef's knife or gyuto, with a steeper curve for rocking cuts.",
      "The nashiji hammered finish helps food release from the blade as well as looking good.",
      "The blade is 20-layer suminagashi steel with a Shiro2 high-carbon core, hardened to about 60-61 Rockwell.",
      "Specs: 11.6 in overall length, a blade of about 6.5 in, 134 g.",
      "The handle is 200-year-old black walnut from McMinnville, banded in Guava Pink and Golden Yolk.",
      "Only 100 are made, by hand in Falls City, Oregon, each with a signed certificate of authenticity."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "The story behind the knife",
        "summary": "Kenji introduces the first production prototype and explains his lifelong love of knives and his wish to design his own.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 0
      },
      {
        "title": "Meeting the bladesmith",
        "summary": "How Kenji met Gordon Hanson while staying at his tiny home rental. He describes forging his first slim-tipped santoku-style knife with Gordon over two days.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "Design and functionality",
        "summary": "The production design is slimmer and lighter than Kenji's first knife. It is a santoku base with a tapered tip and steeper curve, so it can both chop and rock.",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "last_segment": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "Aesthetic features",
        "summary": "The nashiji hammered finish aids food release, and the knife is meant to be a pleasure to hold. The walnut handle carries banding in Kenji's signature colors.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 7
      },
      {
        "title": "Materials and specifications",
        "summary": "Covers the 20-layer suminagashi steel (as opposed to Damascus), the acid etching, the Shiro2 core at 60-61 HRC, and the length and weight.",
        "first_segment": 7,
        "last_segment": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "Production and availability",
        "summary": "Gordon hand-makes a run of 100 in Falls City, Oregon. Each comes with a signed certificate of authenticity, and ordering links are provided.",
        "first_segment": 8,
        "last_segment": 9
      }
